WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:11.980
(Background music)

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.020
Hello everyone, welcome to the Langfocus Channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.980
Today we're going to focus on a language called Catalan.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.000
Catalan is a member of the Occitano-Romance branch of the Romance language family.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.420
It's mainly spoken in and around Catalonia, an autonomous community in Spain,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.000
as well as in another autonomous

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:37.200
community, the Balearic Islands, and also in the autonomous community of Valencia.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.959
But some people will definitely dispute that.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.899
Many members of the Valencian community will insist that they speak a separate language called Valencian.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:52.040
Others, including most linguists, simply consider Valencian to be a collection of Catalan dialects.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.600
WARNING. Abandon topic. Abandon topic.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.000
The comments section is about to blow.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.180
Okay, let's leave that issue aside for now.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.740
But in any case, in all three of those autonomous communities

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.629
Catalan is co-official with Spanish, except in Valencia,

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.960
where it's co-official with Spanish under the name "Valencian".

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.480
Oh man, this video just started and I've already got a headache

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.650
Catalan is also spoken in the tiny principality of Andorra,

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:25.709
which is located in the Pyrenees Mountains, and is bordered by France to the north and Spain to the south.

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:30.569
Catalan is also spoken across the border in France in the northern Catalonia region.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.540
Catalan is closely related to Occitan,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.189
which is spoken in areas of France adjacent to northern Catalonia. In the past

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:42.419
Catalan and Occitan were considered to be dialect groups of the same language

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:48.148
But they're now widely considered to be independent sister languages of the same Occitano-Romance branch.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:52.600
Catalan is also spoken in the town of Alghero in Sardinia, Italy.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.979
Catalan is spoken by over 10 million people, some of whom

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:00.269
Indisputably speak it as a native language, and some of whom speak another language at home

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:06.089
(usually Spanish) but are also fluent in Catalan and speak it at a native level because of their environment.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:12.539
It's the sixth most spoken Romance language after Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian, and Romanian.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:18.280
It's also the most spoken non-state language in Europe, meaning that it's the most spoken language

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.739
that's not an official language at the national level

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:25.780
That's because it's not official at the national level in Spain, even though millions of people speak it.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:31.929
It's only official in Andorra. Some people want it to be the official language of a new country called Catalonia

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.400
that would be separate from Spain.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.000
WARNING

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:38.230
Tell you what,

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.000
you can just google it.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.340
History.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.100
Like all Romance languages,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:47.590
Catalan developed from dialects of vulgar latin in areas that had been under the control of the Roman Empire.

00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:53.200
The earliest form of Occitano-Romance, known as Old Occitan or Old Provençal,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.559
had developed by the 8th century, and later some of its

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:01.400
varieties began to diverge into Old Catalan between the 10th and 14th centuries.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.160
In the 12th century

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:08.319
the county of Barcelona, a Catalan county, formed a dynastic union with the Kingdom of Aragon,

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:13.900
forming the Crown of Aragon, and other Catalan counties formed the Principality of Catalonia

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.739
together with Barcelona, bringing them into the crown. The Crown of Aragon

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.540
expanded under the rule of James the Conqueror

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.419
who brought Valencia and the Balearic Islands under the Crown's control and these became

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:26.900
Catalan speaking areas.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.289
The political capital of the crown of Aragon was the city of Zaragoza in the kingdom of Aragon.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.519
But the de facto economic and cultural centers were Barcelona and Valencia,

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:39.069
both Catalan-speaking cities.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:46.100
By the 14th and 15th centuries, the Crown of Aragon had greatly expanded to include Corsica, Sardinia,

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:51.549
Sicily, and Naples and for a brief time Athens and Neopatras in present-day Greece.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:57.100
There was a large Catalan commercial presence throughout the Mediterranean region, and the Catalan language

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.680
became widespread, at least amongst the commercial class.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.429
Catalan was considered one of the great languages of Europe

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:09.100
But in 1479 the crown of Aragon formed a union with the Crown of Castile, and

00:04:09.109 --> 00:04:12.520
Castilian today also known as just Spanish or espanol

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.418
began to grow in influence and Catalan began to decline in influence

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.940
During much of the period from the 16th century to the 20th century,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.279
various laws were enacted that limited the use of Catalan in

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:29.049
official documents, in publications, in administration and in schools. But since

00:04:29.599 --> 00:04:32.649
1975, it has been made co-official in Catalonia, Valencia,

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:39.849
and the Balearic Islands, and in Catalonia schools are now required to teach most subjects in Catalan. In Valencia

00:04:39.849 --> 00:04:43.989
there's less instruction in Catalan (known officially there as Valencian, by the way),

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.659
but there are current government efforts to increase it.

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:52.929
In the Balearic Islands at least 50% of the students schooling must be conducted in Catalan, though

00:04:52.930 --> 00:05:00.639
There are also programs that are taught entirely in Catalan. All of the Catalan speaking autonomous areas of Spain are bilingual, with Catalan speakers

00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:02.639
also being Spanish speakers.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.810
So what's Catalan like?

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.029
Once you learn one Romance language,

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:13.269
you've already taken a great step towards understanding the others. If we look at a list of basic greetings in Catalan,

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:18.129
there will definitely be some words that speakers of other Romance languages will recognize immediately.

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:20.799
Good morning, or good day: Bon dia.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:28.539
That sounds an awful lot like Portuguese "bom dia" and Spanish "buenos dias". Good afternoon: Bona tarda.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:37.000
Compare that with Portuguese "boa tarde" and Spanish "buenas tardes". Good evening or good night: Bona nit.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:43.329
Compare that with Portuguese "boa noite", and Spanish "buenas noches", and in Italian

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:48.399
"good night" would be "buona notte". To say "bye", you might say "Adéu".

00:05:48.979 --> 00:05:51.800
Compare that to Portuguese "adeus",

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:58.900
Spanish "adiós" and French "adieu". And if you want to be more formal, you might say "A reveure".

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.239
Compare that with French "au revoir".

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.449
Now, let's look at some Catalan sentences and

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:11.409
examine some of the language's basic features, many of which are quite similar to other Romance languages, but with some differences, of course.

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:18.669
"El Josep és un bon professor". This means "Joseph is a good teacher". "El Josep és un bon professor". Word-for-word

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:24.639
it's: the-Joseph-is-a-good-teacher. This sentence follows basically the same word order

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:29.829
you would find in other Romance languages and in English, with one difference: the definite article "el"

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:37.329
before the name Josep. In Catalan a definite article is used before people's first names, and this is called "the personal article".

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.370
We'll come back to definite articles in a minute.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:43.649
This whole sentence is masculine singular. Like other Romance languages,

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:50.820
Catalan nouns, articles, and adjectives are inflected for grammatical gender and number. Let's look at a similar feminine sentence.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.610
"La Isabel és una bona professora". Isabel is a good teacher. "La Isabel és una bona professora".

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:04.890
Feminine nouns generally end in "a", and the adjectives that modify them are inflected with the feminine ending "a" as well, while masculine nouns

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:07.499
generally end in a consonant or an "o" or "i".

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:14.490
One other thing I want to point out here is that the letter "r" is silent in the final position in Catalan.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.279
So this word "professor" ends in a vowel sound.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.940
There are basically four definite articles.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:28.650
The singular forms are "el" and "la", which changed to l' with an apostrophe if the following word starts with a vowel or an "h".

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:32.940
The plural forms are "els" and "les". For the personal article,

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:38.519
there are the alternate forms "en" and "na", n' with an apostrophe before a vowel or an "h".

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:43.349
The indefinite articles are un, una, uns, and unes.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:48.990
The way articles work in Catalan is similar to in other Romance languages, but the forms are different, of course.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:52.710
And there are also some dialectal differences in Catalan articles.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:58.739
Another sentence: "Ella sempre dina molt tard". This means "She always has lunch very late".

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:05.789
"Ella sempre dina molt tard". Word-for-word it's: she-always-has lunch-very-late. One thing

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:10.050
I'd like to point out right off the bat is this phrase "molt tard". In Spanish

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:15.150
it would be "muy tarde". In Portuguese it would be "muito tarde". In Italian

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:21.689
it would be "molto tardi" - quite similar to the Catalan phrase. But in the other languages words tend to end in vowels,

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:26.039
especially in Italian. Words end in consonants much more often in Catalan.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:30.390
But also pay attention to the consonants at the end of these words.

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:37.710
Here we see the simplification of final consonant clusters. "T" is often not pronounced after another consonant at the end of a word,

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:45.329
especially in the combinations of LT, RT, NT, and ST. Sometimes it may be pronounced slightly, or

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:52.650
articulated but not released. And in final position, the letter "d" is pronounced as /t/. But similar to "t",

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:56.220
it's often silent or unreleased after another consonant.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:00.839
Simplification of final consonant clusters is a prominent feature of Catalan

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:08.129
But it does not always happen and it varies depending on the dialect. The speaker in the samples speaks a central Catalan dialect.

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:13.410
Here, we have a personal pronoun as the subject of the sentence. This is actually not necessary.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:21.239
Catalan is a pro dropped language, meaning that the subject pronoun can be dropped because person and number are clear from the verb conjugation.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.359
Like other Romance languages, Catalan has two types of personal pronouns: strong pronouns,

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:34.800
which are used as subject pronouns and after prepositions, the only difference being in the first person singular; and there are weak pronouns,

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:42.629
which are clinics that are used for the direct or indirect object. Catalan has more weak pronoun forms than other major Romance languages.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:46.500
Just a note about this word "dina". In this sentence.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:52.700
It's pronounced as /ðina/ because in Catalan "d" is pronounced as the soft /ð/ sound between vowels.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:58.529
Even though the "d" is in the initial position here, it is affected by the vowel at the end of the previous word.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:06.419
"Dina" is a regular verb meaning "to have lunch", and here it's conjugated in the third-person singular for the present tense.

00:10:07.149 --> 00:10:10.138
Catalan has three categories of regular verbs, which are

00:10:10.269 --> 00:10:15.269
characterized by different endings. Verbs ending in "ar", also known as the first conjugation;

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:21.569
verbs ending in "re" or "er", the second conjugation; and verbs ending in IR, the third

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:26.000
conjugation. Verbs in each category have the same or similar conjugations.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:29.339
Another sentence: "M'ha donat els diners aquest matí".

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:37.230
This means "He gave me the money this morning". "M'ha donat els diners aquest matí." Word-for-word it's: to me-has given- the-

00:10:37.509 --> 00:10:39.568
masculine plural-money-this-morning.

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.389
The first word m' is a weak pronoun,

00:10:42.389 --> 00:10:49.948
which is used for the indirect object here. This form of the pronoun m' comes before vowels and the letter h, while before

00:10:50.199 --> 00:10:52.600
consonants it's "em", as in E-M.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:59.850
Notice that it comes before the verb. "ha donat" is the perfect form of the verb "donar". The perfect form includes the auxiliary

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.600
verb "haver" followed by the past participle.

00:11:02.769 --> 00:11:09.779
It's used for events in the recent past, and events within timeframes that are connected to now, like "this month" or "this year".

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:17.670
There are parallel tenses in other Romance languages, and Catalan's system of verb tenses in general is very similar to other Romance languages.

00:11:18.069 --> 00:11:21.659
"Donat" is another example of a Catalan word ending in a consonant

00:11:22.089 --> 00:11:27.929
where it would end with a vowel in other Romance languages. In Italian, this would be "donato" and in Spanish

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:30.149
it would be "donado". In Romanian, however,

00:11:30.149 --> 00:11:37.558
it would be "donat". Next, notice the masculine plural definite article, because the word for money "diners" is plural.

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:43.900
The word "aquest" is similar to Italian "questa" or Spanish and Portuguese "esta",

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.499
But notice that the "s" and "t" are pronounced differently. The "s" is silent and the "t" is not released.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:56.699
"Matí" is similar to Italian "mattina" or French "matin". Coming back to the weak pronouns for a minute,

00:11:56.699 --> 00:11:59.818
what if we want to use a pronoun in place of "els diners"?

00:12:00.399 --> 00:12:08.000
"M'ho ha donat aquest matí." The direct object, in this case "ho" meaning "it", must come after the indirect object (m').

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:14.800
Weak pronouns come after the verb in the case of imperatives, at least positive imperatives, gerunds, and infinitives.

00:12:14.829 --> 00:12:19.500
So the imperative sentence "give it to me" would be “Dóna-m'ho”.

00:12:19.839 --> 00:12:26.039
Another sentence: "(Ell) es va graduar fa dos anys." This means he graduated two years ago. Word-for-word

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:30.089
it's: reflexive pronoun-he graduated-makes-two-years.

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:33.899
Remember that the subject pronoun "ell" is probably not necessary.

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:38.040
Let's look at the end of this sentence first. This phrase here "fa dos anys"

00:12:38.259 --> 00:12:44.219
shows the use of the verb "fer", meaning "to make", to tell how much time has passed since an event happened.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:48.779
This is similar to how it would be expressed in Italian: "due anni fa".

00:12:49.569 --> 00:12:57.029
“Es va graduar” is a past tense construction called "the periphrastic past" that is unique to Catalan. It consists of an auxilary verb

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:04.410
related to, but not identical to, the present tense of the verb "anar" meaning to go, followed by a verb in the infinitive.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:10.379
The periphrastic past is identical in meaning to the simple past, which is now normally limited to literary use.

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:16.319
Note that the reflexive pronoun (in a different form) can appear after the verb instead ,if it's an

00:13:16.389 --> 00:13:19.709
infinitive or an imperative or gerund. So this could also be

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:26.000
"Va graduar-se" and the whole sentence could be "(Ell) va graduar-se fa dos anys."

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:31.019
This verb construction is somewhat surprising to me because it looks quite similar to the periphrastic

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.120
future using "anar" (to go).

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:39.959
"“Va a graduar-se” means "He is going to graduate" with "va" being the third-person singular present tense of "anar".

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:48.360
This periphrastic future form is used less in Catalan than in some other Romance languages probably because it resembles the periphrastic past.

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:53.879
So instead the simple future can be used. "Es graduarà". "He will graduate".

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:59.500
In Catalan you typically form the simple future tense by adding a suffix to the infinitive form.

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:04.070
But for some irregular verbs there is a different future stem not the infinitive.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:11.960
In general, the way that Catalan verbs are conjugated is quite similar to other Romance languages except that the exact forms are somewhat different.

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:16.789
With Western Romance languages in general, if you speak one or if you've studied one,

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:22.700
then the other seems somewhat familiar right off the bat. But on the other hand everything seems somewhat different as well.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:30.559
There's some different vocabulary, different phonology, some different grammatical features, and sometimes things are just expressed differently. Being previously

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:38.150
unfamiliar with Catalan for the most part, what strikes me is that I notice features that are similar to Spanish, to French, to Italian,

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:39.770
to Portuguese, and to Romanian.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.700
But I would never confuse it with one of those languages

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:46.340
because it has its own unique elements as well. The question of the day. For

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:51.019
speakers of Catalan: What other Romance languages do you find closest to Catalan?

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:55.460
Can you communicate with Occitan speakers easily or Italian speakers, etc

00:14:55.460 --> 00:15:01.970
And for speakers of other Romance languages: How familiar does Catalan seem to you? What differences stand out to you?

